README-GCC -- using the gcc toolchains on this diskVerified 2026-08-24 by compiling, linking and RUNNING a C program and a C++program on this disk.  Both work.  Neither works out of the box, and thisfile is the difference.WHICH ONE -- THE SHORT ANSWER  Take GCC2 unless your machine is short of memory.  You cannot have both  installed at once (their C++ passes want the same name), so this is a  choice you have to make, and it comes down to two things.  GCC2 is GNU C 2.5.6 with a 2.5.8 C++; GCC139 is GNU C 1.39 with a 1.37.1  C++.  Four years apart.  Neither is a version you MUST have: both compile  ordinary C, and nothing on this disk needs one rather than the other.  MEMORY is the reason to want the older one.  Measured from the files:      the C path       driver + preprocessor + compiler        GCC2            1085 KB        GCC139           563 KB          -- about half      the C++ path        GCC2            1463 KB        GCC139           890 KB  On a machine where a 1085 KB process is a lot, GCC139 compiles C in half the  space.  That is the whole of the case for it.  THE LANGUAGE is the reason to want the newer one.  1.39 predates ANSI C  being settled and its C++ is 1.37.1 -- no templates, no exceptions, and a  long way from anything written after about 1992.  If you are compiling  code from the era this disk collects, either will do; if you are  compiling something newer, only 2.5.6 has a chance.  Backward compatibility runs the way you would expect: code that 1.39  compiles, 2.5.6 compiles too.  The reverse is not true.  Neither can finish a build on its own -- see the next section but one.WHAT IS HERE  /dd/CMDS/GCC2      GNU C 2.5.6.  gcc2 drives C, gpp drives C++.  The                     passes are cccp2, cc2, cc1plus, cc2plus and collect.  /dd/CMDS/GCC139    GNU C 1.39, the same shape, passes already named                     gcc_cc1, gcc_cccp, gcc_cc1plus, gcc_collect.  /dd/GNULIB         os9lib.l, gnulib.l, gpp.l, libgpp.l  /dd/DEFS/GCC2      the gcc header set  /dd/DEFS/os9lib    the C headers matching GNULIB/os9lib.l  /dd/SRC/gcc142     the source of GCC2's gcc driver, which reports                     `gcc version 1.42' -- the OS-9 port of GNU C 1.42THE SOURCE FOR THESE BINARIES, AND WHERE IT IS NOT  GCC is under the GNU General Public Licence, which is about SOURCE: what  you receive, you may pass on, and whoever receives it should be able to  get the source.  So this file says plainly what is here and what is not.  HERE:    SRC/gcc142        the source of GCC2's `gcc' driver, which reports                      "gcc version 1.42" -- the OS-9 port of GNU C 1.42    SRC/vh            VH, the hypertext browser shipped as `jargon'  NOT HERE, and we have looked:    gcc 1.39 and its passes            CMDS/GCC139    g++ 1.40.3 (gpp, cc1plus)          CMDS/GCC2, CMDS/GCC139    gcc 2.5.6 (gcc2, cc2, cccp2)       CMDS/GCC2    cc2plus 2.5.8                      CMDS/GCC2  These arrived as binaries in the OS-9 archives that kept them, and no  copy of the matching source has been found in any archive this project  has been able to reach.  The 2.5.6 archive does at least ship the GPL  itself as `copying'; the 1.x archives carry no licence text at all.  What upstream source exists is not secret: GNU C 1.39, 1.40, 1.42 and  2.5.6 are the FSF's own releases and survive in the GNU archives.  What  is missing is the OS-9 PORT of them -- the changes that made them build  and run here -- and that is what nobody has published.  One piece of it has turned up.  comp.sources.misc v13i005 to v13i011, May  1990, is Mr. Seyama's port of GCC 1.37 to OS-9/68000 -- diffs against the  stock FSF 1.37 release, with documentation in English, posted for him by  NIIMI Makoto of Keio University.  It is not the version of any binary  here and one of its seven parts is missing from the archive it was  recovered from, so it does not close the question; it is recorded because  it is the only OS-9 GCC source this project has ever found, and the next  person looking should start there rather than from nothing.  If you have the source for any of the four above, this collection would  like it.WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND CANNOT BE  r68 (assembler), l68 (linker), clibn.l and cstart.r are Microware's and  are not in this collection.  gcc compiles C to ASSEMBLY and then hands  that to r68, and hands the objects to l68.  Without those two you can  preprocess and compile and get no further.  So: you need your own OS-9 SDK.  Nothing on this disk can replace it,  and the six assemblers here (as0, as09, as1, as4, as5, as11) will not  do -- they are 6800, 6809, 6801, 6804, 6805 and 68HC11 cross-assemblers,  not 68000 ones.HOW THE DRIVERS FIND THEIR PASSES -- THE SURPRISE  gcc2 and gpp do not look in the directory they were started from.  Each  has two prefixes compiled into it, and it looks ONLY there:      gcc2   /dd/CMDS/gcc_<pass>    and  /h0/CMDS/gcc_<pass>      gpp    /dd/CMDS/gpp_<pass>    and  /h0/CMDS/gpp_<pass>  Note that they are DIFFERENT prefixes.  Anything else a driver runs --  r68, l68, del -- it forks by bare name, which OS-9 looks up in the  EXECUTION directory, normally /dd/CMDS.  The passes ship in /dd/CMDS/GCC2, which is none of those places, so a  bare attempt stops immediately:      $ /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gcc2 -c hello.c      gcc2: Can't fork 'cccp2': 000:221 module not found  That is not a broken binary.  It is a program looking where it was told  to look and not finding anything.WHAT TO DO  1.  Put your SDK's r68 and l68 where a bare name is found -- /dd/CMDS,      or /h0/CMDS if you would rather keep this disk read-only.  2.  Nothing.  The five copies this used to ask you to make ARE NOW ON      THE DISK, installed 2026-08-27:          /dd/CMDS/gcc_cccp2      gcc2's preprocessor          /dd/CMDS/gcc_cc2        gcc2's C compiler proper          /dd/CMDS/gpp_cccp       the same preprocessor under gpp's name          /dd/CMDS/gpp_cc1plus    gpp's C++ compiler proper          /dd/CMDS/gpp_collect    gpp's global-constructor collector      cccp2 is present twice on purpose: gcc2 forks it as cccp2 and gpp      forks it as cccp.  The originals are still in /dd/CMDS/GCC2; these      are byte copies, so the two directories cannot disagree.      GCC139 is deliberately NOT installed this way.  Its passes are      already named gcc_cc1, gcc_cccp, gcc_cc1plus and gcc_collect, and      gcc_cc1plus would collide with GCC2's.  To use GCC139 instead, copy      /dd/CMDS/GCC139's passes into /dd/CMDS yourself and remove GCC2's.  3.  Put clibn.l and cstart.r where l68 will find them -- /dd/LIB or      /h0/LIB.  Then both of these work:      /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gcc2 -o hello hello.c      /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gpp  -o hello hello.cc  GCC139 cannot be installed at the same time as GCC2 this way: its  gcc_cc1plus and GCC2's would want the same name.  Pick one.HEADERS  <stdio.h> in /dd/DEFS/GCC2 forwards to /dd/DEFS/os9lib/stdio.h, which is  the header that matches GNULIB/os9lib.l, the C library this disk ships  for gcc.  It used to forward to /dd/DEFS/stdio.h, which is Microware's  and is not on this disk, so nothing that included <stdio.h> could be  compiled at all.  Fixed 2026-08-24.  os9lib's header has no SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR or SEEK_END; DEFS/GCC2/stdio.h  adds them.ONE COSMETIC FAILURE YOU WILL SEE  gpp removes its temporary files by forking `del', and this disk has rm  and no del.  You will see      gpp: Program del got fatal signal 221.  AFTER your program has already been written.  The output is fine; the  temporaries are not cleaned up.  Supply a del from your SDK if it  bothers you.